  "text": "Samples of a light metal‑precious metal alloy that had been allowed to reach equilibrium were heated from room temperature to above the temperature where two phases coexist. While heating, the changes in heat capacity and electrical conductivity were recorded. The characteristic shifts that occur when the precipitated phase dissolves were used to map the solubility limit of the precious metal in the light metal. The resulting solubility curve shows a distinct bend near a composition of about 20 atomic percent of the precious metal at roughly 526 °C, confirming an earlier microscopic observation of this feature. In addition, the enthalpy and entropy associated with forming the solid solution were calculated for compositions ranging from 1.3 to 50 atomic percent and plotted. Both plots display a curvature that is likely linked to the electronic band structure of the pure light metal. Using a theoretical density‑of‑states model, the energy of the valence electrons in the solutions was estimated, suggesting that the light metal contributes about 2.5 electrons per atom rather than three."
